{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the turbulent personal relationships that fueled its creation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie doesn’t just tell a story about technology—it dissects ambition, betrayal, and the cost of genius in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached, a man who builds a global empire while remaining profoundly isolated. his portrayal is chillingly authentic, never resorting to caricature, instead offering a quiet, intense intensity that lingers long after the credits roll.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is nothing short of brilliant. every line crackles with wit, rhythm, and intellectual heft. sorkin’s signature rapid-fire dialogue turns legal depositions into gripping drama, transforming courtroom scenes into psychological showdowns. the structure, alternating between two lawsuits and the timeline of facebook’s founding, keeps the narrative tight and electric, never letting the audience lose momentum.\n\njoseph gordon-levitt and andrew garfield shine as sean parker and justin timberlake, respectively, each bringing charisma and complexity to their roles. parker, in particular, is a force of chaotic energy—a digital pied piper who sees the future before anyone else, but whose flaws are as vast as his vision. the supporting cast, including armie hammer as the winklevoss twins, is flawless; hammer’s dual performance is so seamless it’s easy to forget you’re watching one actor.\n\ntrent reznor and atticus ross’s score is haunting and minimalist, a perfect sonic companion to the film’s cold, digital aesthetic. the pulsing synths and ambient tones mirror the isolation of the characters and the relentless pace of innovation. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth enhances the mood—cool blues and grays dominate, reflecting the sterile environments of dorm rooms and boardrooms, where human connections are reduced to code and contracts.\n\nbeneath the surface, the film is a tragedy. it’s not about whether facebook was stolen, but about what was lost in its creation: friendships, trust, and humanity itself. the final scene—mark refreshing his friend count, alone in his dorm room—is one of the most poignant moments in modern cinema. it says more about loneliness in the age of connection than any dialogue could.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a biopic—it’s a cultural artifact. it captures the moment when the internet shifted from a tool to a force that reshapes identity, power, and relationships. it’s smart, fast, emotionally resonant, and devastatingly accurate in its portrayal of modern ambition. even a decade later, it feels more relevant than ever.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005541696446016431}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.3317681550979614}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.6493244767189026}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0007980260998010635}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003777275327593088}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0001299296854995191}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -0.7686036825180054}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.3947389561508317e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.7288458347320557}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.12696930766105652}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00035279724397696555}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.34996578097343445}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.9907753085135482e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -1.1555